  "summary": "Capcom’s arena fighter turns furniture, space and three transforming gems into the tools of a two-player duel.",
  "body": [
    "Power Stone is Capcom’s three-dimensional arena fighting game, first released in Japanese arcades on 13 February 1999 and brought to Dreamcast later that month. Its adventurers search for legendary stones said to grant a wish, meeting in colourful rooms and outdoor arenas along the way. Each duel allows the fighters to run around the space, jump towards an opponent and turn objects into weapons. A table or chair becomes part of the encounter, giving the room a more active role than a decorative backdrop.",
    "Three Power Stones allow a fighter to transform into a much stronger version of themselves. Gathering them changes the immediate purpose of movement: reaching a loose stone can become more urgent than landing another ordinary hit. The transformed character gains powerful attacks, but the advantage lasts only while a separate meter remains. Spending it on a special move creates a short burst of power. The opponent must then decide whether to contest the stones, keep away for a moment or risk closing the distance.",
    "The Dreamcast adaptation brought this compact arcade duel into a home setting suited to repeated matches. Players could learn an arena’s furniture and routes, then watch a familiar room produce a different exchange when someone reached a stone first. Tetsuya Shibata’s soundtrack accompanies a cast with exaggerated transformations and contrasting fighting styles. The first game concentrates on two opponents, leaving the space readable enough to follow their pursuit around the room. Power Stone 2 would build on that foundation with four-player battles and changing stages."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A fight across the room",
    "Three stones change the balance",
    "A Dreamcast gathering"
  ],
